/// \defgroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks Debug integer streams, the throw hook and the debugger probe
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief The `Debug` stream operators for integers and `bool`, a hook that
///        reports C++ throws through the exception report, a debugger
///        probe, and the benchmark's fatal-exit helper that shares their
///        directory.
///
/// These functions sit in the WWDebug directory beside the EA debug
/// library described in \ref grp_w1_debug_lib, which documents the `Debug`
/// object itself, its other stream operators and the report flow. They fall
/// into four parts:
///
/// - **Integer and `bool` stream operators.** They turn a value into text
///   and append it to the report being built, as the string, pointer and
///   floating-point operators do. Integers are written in the current radix,
///   after the current prefix, both chosen by the radix manipulators
///   (`Debug::Hex`, `Debug::Dec` and `Debug::Bin`). Debug::CrashBegin writes
///   a report's line number with Debug::operator<<(int), and
///   DebugExceptionhandler::LogRegisters writes the processor registers in
///   hexadecimal and the flags in binary through the unsigned overload.
///   In BFME 2 each of these operators is a virtual function of the `Debug`
///   object, like the string, floating-point and pointer writers; Zero
///   Hour's `Debug` declares none of its stream operators virtual.
/// - **The RaiseException import hook.** InstallRaiseExceptionHook patches
///   `msvcrt.dll`'s import of `RaiseException` so that RaiseExceptionHook
///   sees each exception raised through it, the way a C++ `throw` is raised.
///   Once installed, whenever the `Debug` object's windowed query says yes,
///   it sends the exception to the exception report, which ends the game
///   (see RaiseExceptionHook). Only when nothing is reported is the
///   exception forwarded to the original `RaiseException`.
/// - **The debugger probe.** DebugIsDebuggerAttached tells the library
///   whether a debugger is attached, which changes how crash reports and
///   dialogs behave.
/// - **ErrorExit**, which is not part of the debug library: it is the BYTEmark
///   (nbench) benchmark's fatal-error exit, called from the benchmark tests
///   that W3DShaderManager::testMinimumRequirements runs. See
///   \ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty for the benchmark.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Nothing here reads game data or holds game state, and none of it takes
/// part in the logic simulation, saves or replays. Debug commands (for
/// example from a `.dbgcmd` command file run at start-up, or typed into the
/// debug console; see \ref grp_w1_debug_lib) include two controls that
/// affect this group:
/// - `debug.skipstackwalk +` or `-` sets the same flag the debugger probe
///   sets at start-up (see DebugIsDebuggerAttached);
/// - `debug.windowed +` or `-` forces the `Debug` object's windowed query on
///   or off, and that query decides whether RaiseExceptionHook, once
///   installed, reports a throw.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - The hook and the probe are Win32 seams: the hook patches a DLL import
///   table and reads registers with x86 inline assembly, and the probe asks
///   `kernel32` about debuggers. A port can drop the hook and replace the
///   probe with the platform's own check.
/// - The stream operators format with the Microsoft C runtime's `_itoa`,
///   `_ultoa`, `_i64toa` and `_ui64toa`, which are Microsoft extensions, not
///   standard C or C++; a port to another runtime may need replacements.
///
/// \see \ref grp_w1_debug_lib, \ref sub_common_services_debug


/// \file WWDebug/DebugRaiseExceptionHook.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief The RaiseException import hook and its installer.


/// \file WWDebug/DebugIsDebuggerAttached.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief The debug library's debugger probe.


/// \file WWDebug/ErrorExit.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief The BYTEmark benchmark's fatal-error exit, kept beside the debug
///        library sources.


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(int val)
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief Appends a signed integer in the current radix, after the current
///        prefix.
///
/// The debug library's own reports use it; for example, Debug::CrashBegin
/// writes the reporting site's line number with it, and Debug::CrashDone
/// writes a site's hit count.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The prefix (for example `0x` after `Debug::Hex`, a percent sign after
///   `Debug::Bin`, nothing after `Debug::Dec`) is appended directly with
///   Debug::AddOutput. The radix starts at 10 when the `Debug` object is
///   constructed.
/// - The digits come from the C runtime's `_itoa` in a small stack buffer
///   and are written through Debug::operator<<(const char *). A field width
///   (`Debug::Width`) and fill character therefore pad the digits but not
///   the prefix: with fill `0`, width 8 and `Debug::Hex`, a value prints as
///   `0x` followed by eight digits.
/// - In radix 10 a negative value gets a minus sign; in any other radix
///   `_itoa` prints the value's 32-bit two's-complement pattern. Letters in
///   digits are lowercase.
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. In BFME 2 the operator is virtual and is
///       called through the object's virtual function table. The `long`
///       overload compiles to identical code, so retail keeps one body for
///       both and both table slots point to it.


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(unsigned val)
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief Appends an unsigned integer in the current radix, after the
///        current prefix.
///
/// DebugExceptionhandler::LogRegisters prints the processor registers of
/// an exception report through this body (as `unsigned long` values, which
/// share it), zero-padded in hexadecimal, and the flags register in binary.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same as Debug::operator<<(int), with the C runtime's `_ultoa` in
/// place of `_itoa`, so there is never a sign. The prefix is appended
/// directly with Debug::AddOutput; the digits go through
/// Debug::operator<<(const char *), so a field width pads the digits only.
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \note The body is Zero Hour's. In BFME 2 the operator is virtual, and the
///       `unsigned long` overload, which compiles to identical code, shares
///       this body.
/// \see Debug::operator<<(int), Debug::operator<<(unsigned long long)


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(bool val)
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief Appends `true` or `false`.
///
/// The word is written through Debug::operator<<(const char *), so a field
/// width and fill character apply to it as to any string. No prefix is
/// written and the radix does not matter.
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \note In BFME 2 this operator is virtual, like the other stream
///       operators documented here (Zero Hour's are not); it writes the word
///       through the string operator's virtual function table slot. The body
///       is Zero Hour's.


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(long long val)
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief Appends a signed 64-bit integer in the current radix, after the
///        current prefix.
///
/// The source declares the parameter with MSVC's `__int64` type, which
/// this guide's build reads as `long long`.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The 64-bit form of Debug::operator<<(int): the prefix goes out directly
/// with Debug::AddOutput, then the C runtime's `_i64toa` formats the value
/// into a stack buffer that is written through
/// Debug::operator<<(const char *). As with `_itoa`, only radix 10 prints a
/// minus sign; other radices print the 64-bit two's-complement pattern.
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \note In BFME 2 the operator is virtual, like the other stream operators
///       documented here. The body follows Zero Hour's; BFME 1's copy served
///       as the donor for the reconstruction.
/// \see Debug::operator<<(unsigned long long)


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(unsigned long long val)
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_wwdebug_stream_hooks
/// \brief Appends an unsigned 64-bit integer in the current radix, after the
///        current prefix.
///
/// The source declares the parameter as `unsigned __int64`, which this
/// guide's build reads as `unsigned long long`.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same as the signed 64-bit overload above, with the C runtime's
/// `_ui64toa` in place of `_i64toa`, so there is never a sign.
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \note In BFME 2 the operator is virtual, like the other stream operators
///       documented here. The body follows Zero Hour's.


/// \fn void RaiseExceptionHook(DWORD code, DWORD flags, DWORD numArgs, const DWORD *args)
/// \brief Replacement for `RaiseException` in `msvcrt.dll`'s import table:
///        when the windowed query says yes, sends the exception to the
///        exception report, which ends the process; otherwise forwards it
///        to the original `RaiseException`.
///
/// InstallRaiseExceptionHook writes this function's address into the
/// import slot, so it is called with `RaiseException`'s arguments and
/// calling convention whenever code in `msvcrt.dll` raises an exception.
/// The Microsoft C++ runtime raises every C++ `throw` this way, so the hook
/// sees throws from code that uses that runtime DLL.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Only when the `Debug` object's windowed query says yes does it report
///   anything; otherwise it only forwards. Unless `debug.windowed` overrides
///   it, that query treats the game as windowed when the calling thread's
///   first window has a caption bar or is taller than it is wide, and as
///   not windowed otherwise, caching the answer either way. Until it has
///   found a window and cached an answer, it also says yes when the calling
///   thread owns no window.
/// - It builds an exception record whose only field is the Microsoft C++
///   exception code (the code a C++ `throw` raises). That code is used even
///   when the caller raised a different one, because the caller's code,
///   flags and arguments are not copied in. It also builds a processor
///   context that holds only the hook's own instruction, frame and stack
///   pointers, read with inline assembly. The report's stack walk therefore
///   starts inside the hook.
/// - It passes both to DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter. After that
///   call, and directly when the windowed query says no, the code calls the
///   original `RaiseException` saved by the installer with the unchanged
///   arguments; see the warning below for when that call is reached.
///
/// \param code    Exception code the caller raises; forwarded unchanged.
/// \param flags   Exception flags; forwarded unchanged.
/// \param numArgs Number of entries in `args`; forwarded unchanged.
/// \param args    Exception arguments, or null; forwarded unchanged.
///
/// \warning In BFME 2, DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter ends the
///          process unless it is re-entered, so whenever the windowed query
///          says yes, the first throw that reaches the hook ends the game
///          with an exception report, even a throw that the code would have
///          caught. The forwarding call is reached only when the windowed
///          query says no, or when the filter returns, which it does only
///          when re-entered (it then shows a message box and returns at
///          once).
/// \note Retail quirk, read from the matched body's code rather than
///       observed at run time: the saved original is called as if the
///       caller removed the arguments, although `RaiseException` removes its
///       own. If `RaiseException` returns, which happens only when the
///       exception is continued rather than unwound (a C++ throw normally
///       never returns there), the hook pops its saved EDI from the wrong
///       stack slot, inside its own locals. Its frame-pointer epilogue then
///       restores the stack pointer, so only EDI, a register the caller
///       expects to be preserved, comes back wrong.
/// \see InstallRaiseExceptionHook, Debug::InstallExceptionHandler


/// \fn bool InstallRaiseExceptionHook()
/// \brief Redirects `msvcrt.dll`'s import of `RaiseException` to
///        RaiseExceptionHook.
///
/// BFME 1's `Debug` constructor calls it right after setting the
/// unhandled-exception filter. BFME 2's constructor no longer makes that
/// call; whether anything else in BFME 2 installs the hook is not
/// established here (`tools/callers_of.py` lists the callers the project
/// knows of).
///
/// \par Implementation
/// 1. Looks up `RaiseException` in `kernel32.dll` with `GetProcAddress`.
/// 2. Finds the already loaded `msvcrt.dll` with `GetModuleHandleA`; it
///    does not load the DLL. It checks that the handle is readable, the
///    module's DOS and PE signatures, and that the import-descriptor
///    pointer does not work out equal to the PE header pointer.
/// 3. Walks the module's import descriptors and, in each, the import
///    address table, looking for a slot that holds the `RaiseException`
///    address.
/// 4. In the first such slot it saves the original address for the hook,
///    makes the page writable with `VirtualProtect`, writes the hook's
///    address and restores the old protection.
///
/// \return True when a slot was patched. False when `RaiseException` cannot
///         be found, `msvcrt.dll` is not loaded (its handle fails a read
///         check), its DOS or PE signature is wrong, its import-directory
///         entry fails the installer's sanity check (the descriptor pointer
///         equals the PE header pointer), or no slot holds the address, as
///         is normally the case for a second call after a successful one.
///
/// \note Inference from the code, not observed at run time: only
///       `msvcrt.dll`'s imports are patched. The game's own C runtime imports
///       come from `MSVCR71.dll`, which raises its C++ exceptions through its
///       own import of `RaiseException`, so throws from the game's own code
///       do not appear to pass through the hook.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Import-table patching of a specific DLL, with 32-bit pointers stored as
/// `DWORD` values. A port that wants throw reporting needs a different
/// mechanism, such as the platform's exception or crash-handler API.
/// \see RaiseExceptionHook


/// \fn bool DebugIsDebuggerAttached()
/// \brief Asks Windows whether a debugger is attached to the game process.
///
/// Callers include:
/// - the `Debug` constructor, which sets the library's skip-stack-walk flag
///   when a debugger is attached. Debug::CrashDone then leaves the stack
///   walk out of non-fatal crash reports (fatal ones always walk), and
///   Debug::AssertDone does the same for non-fatal assert reports. The
///   `debug.skipstackwalk` command changes the same flag later;
/// - DebugStackwalk::StackWalk, which falls back to scanning raw stack
///   memory for return addresses when the regular walk finds too few
///   frames and its caller allows the fallback, but only when no debugger
///   is attached;
/// - the assert and crash dialog's window procedure, which hides one of the
///   dialog's controls unless a debugger is attached.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Loads `kernel32` by name and looks up `IsDebuggerPresent` and
/// `CheckRemoteDebuggerPresent` at run time, so a missing function simply
/// counts as "no". The answer is true if either reports a debugger; the
/// remote check asks about the current process.
///
/// \return True when a debugger is attached.
///
/// \note BFME 1's version asks `IsDebuggerPresent` only; the
///       `CheckRemoteDebuggerPresent` query is new in BFME 2. Zero Hour's
///       debug library has no such probe.
/// \note The name is descriptive, chosen by the reconstruction; no original
///       symbol is known.
/// \see DebugStackwalk::StackWalk


/// \fn void ErrorExit()
/// \brief The BYTEmark (nbench) benchmark's fatal-error exit: ends the game
///        process with exit status 1.
///
/// Despite its place in the WWDebug directory, this is nbench's `ErrorExit`
/// from its system-specific helpers, which simply calls `exit(1)`. Its
/// callers in BFME 2 include the benchmark's tests (the numeric and string
/// sorts, bitfield, floating-point emulation, Fourier, assignment, IDEA,
/// Huffman, neural-net and LU tests) and the string-sort array loader. They
/// call it when a test cannot allocate its memory, when a test's
/// self-calibration fails (for example, a sort that still runs too briefly
/// with the most arrays it allows), or, in the neural-net test, when the
/// training data built into the game cannot be parsed.
///
/// The game runs the benchmark through `RunBenchmark` from
/// W3DShaderManager::testMinimumRequirements, its hardware check
/// (\ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty_connects).
///
/// \par Remastering
/// A benchmark failure ends the whole game during hardware detection
/// through the C runtime's `exit`, which runs `atexit` handlers and static
/// destructors but not the engine's normal shutdown. A port that keeps the
/// benchmark should turn these exits into an error result the caller can
/// handle.
/// \see \ref sub_wwlib_thirdparty_vendored
